  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}$ and the point $P(7,\\frac{3}{4})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(\\frac{3}{4},7).$",
  "worked_solution": "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(7,\\frac{3}{4}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x-3}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-\\frac{3}{4(x-3)^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(7) & =-\\frac{3}{32}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(7,\\frac{3}{4})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{3}{4},7)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(\\frac{3}{4}) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{4}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{32})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
